decision_conditions:
The development shall not commence until a Construction Traffic Management Plan has been submitted to and approved in writing by the local planning authority. The statement should include:

a)	photographic condition survey of public carriageways, verges and footways in the vicinity of the site; 
b)	map showing routeing of demolition and construction vehicles to/from the site;
The development shall be carried out in accordance with the approved construction management plan unless otherwise agreed by the Local Planning Authority.

Reason: To ensure construction does not lead to damage of the nearby public road network and to minimise disruption to the neighbouring properties.

Prior to the commencement of the development hereby approved (including demolition and all preparatory work), a scheme for the protection of all retained trees compliant with BS 5837:2012 shall be submitted to and approved in writing by the Local Planning Authority. The scheme will comprise a detailed Arboricultural Method Statement (AMS) report supplemented by Tree Protection Plans (TPP) for both demolition and construction stages. The plans shall be of legible scale and clearly show the layout (as agreed), all relevant existing site features that form a consideration within the protection scheme, and the location/extent of all appropriate protection measures.

Specific matters to be addressed within the AMS and TPPs will include:

a)	methods of demolition of existing structures and boundary treatments within or directly adjacent to Root Protection Areas (RPA - as defined within BS 5837:2012); 
b)	removal of existing hard standing and kerb edging within or directly adjacent to RPAs;
c)	details of all construction that necessitate level changes within RPAs;
d)	details and specification of foundations within RPAs and their installation methodology;
e)	details and specification of all utility services/drainage/SUDs features within RPAs (where unavoidable) and their installation methodology;
f)	a full specification for the construction of all, paths, parking areas and driveways (including alterations to the existing layout of internal hard standing) within RPAs. Where a no-dig sub-base is specified (e.g. cellular confinement system) a specification shall be provided (including a diagrammatical cross-section of the product in-situ) and extent of areas where this would be implemented illustrated within the TPP;
g)	detailed levels and cross-sections to show that the raised levels of surfacing where the installation of no-dig surfacing within RPAs is proposed, demonstrating that the raised areas can be effectively integrated with prevailing levels where they meet with adjacent surfacing or building damp proof courses;
h)	a full specification for the installation of boundary treatments within or directly adjacent to RPAs;
i)	a specification for protective fencing and, if necessary, ground protection, to safeguard all trees during both demolition and construction phases. The Tree Protection Plan(s) shall clearly indicate the alignment of the fencing and extent of ground protection;
j)	a specification for scaffolding and ground protection in Construction Exclusion Zones (where necessary); 
k)	details of site access, temporary parking, on site welfare facilities, loading, unloading and storage of equipment, materials, fuels and waste as well concrete mixing and siting of fires;

The development thereafter shall be implemented in strict accordance with the approved details.

Reason: Required prior to commencement of development to satisfy the Local Planning Authority that the trees to be retained will not be damaged during demolition or construction and to protect and enhance the appearance and character of the site and locality, in accordance with Policy DMD80 (Trees on Development Sites) and pursuant to section 197 of the Town and Country Planning Act 1990.
